# UI: match existing input widths

Do not leave text inputs, selects, or similar controls stretching across the page. Full-width fields are a UI no-no except inside an already-narrow container (modal, sidebar, or a stack that already has `maw`).

**Before adding or laying out a field, probe for an existing fixed-width pattern and reuse it.** Do not invent a new width when a nearby one exists.

1. Search similar screens for `maw=`, `max-width`, and form wrappers (Management sections use `Box maw={560}`; auth stacks use `maw={400}`; short selects use `maw={240}`).
2. Wrap the field group in that same cap (`Box maw={…}` or the existing CSS class). Do not stretch to the desktop main pane.
3. Size the control to its content: short selects and numeric fields stay narrow; labels and help text can fill the capped column.
4. `w="100%"` is fine **inside** a capped container (including mobile). It is not a reason to omit `maw` on desktop.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — input spans the whole main column
<TextInput label="Column label" value={label} onChange={…} />

// ✅ GOOD — same cap as Management form sections
<Box maw={560}>
  <TextInput label="Column label" value={label} onChange={…} />
</Box>
```
